---
extras: ["roads"]
title: Voronoi cells
---
Scatter seed points and give every tile to its nearest seed: the map falls into cells [[t:121-126 s:15]].

## The rule
```text
scatter seed points over the map
each tile joins its nearest seed, by a chosen measure of distance
a tile is on an edge where its right or lower neighbor belongs to another seed
edges become walls (the talk's demo), or roads (our rule)
```

The first use the talk gives for the cells is to find their edges and place walls there [[t:136-138 s:16]]. Its demo draws the edges as `#`, the glyph its shared map code treats as open [[c08a:96-113 backend:54-65]]. We follow the talk’s words and make them walls [[own]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=6

## Controls
@@control seeds
How many seed points are scattered [[c08a:21-27]].
@@control metric
The distance that decides a tile’s nearest seed. The demo shows all three and builds its walls from the straight-line one [[c08a:36-53 c08a:57-73 c08a:77-93 c08a:96-110]].
@@control edges
`walls` is the talk’s demo. `roads` is our own rule for the roads the talk describes: the edges become floor and the cells solid, and a tile is also on an edge when its lower-right neighbor belongs to another seed [[own]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

Walls seal each cell: a map has {{c08a.regions.median}} regions at the median, and the probe finds no path on any of the {{c08a.path.nulls}} seeds [[run:c08a.regions.median c08a.path.nulls]]. The talk’s next use for cells is placing monsters: together in one cell, or kept apart in distant ones [[t:139-141]]. Our roads are one region on {{roads.regions.is1}} of 200 seeds and two on the other {{roads.regions.is2}} [[run:roads.regions.is1 roads.regions.is2]]. Without the lower-right neighbor, an edge that runs down and to the left touches itself only at corners, which seals a cell but can’t be walked [[own]].

## Where it fails
@@fails

These are failures only for a map meant to be walked whole; sealed cells are what the wall rule is for [[own]].

## What it combines with
@@combines

The talk’s author used the edges as roads in a game and filled each cell with a kind of district [[t:142-145]].

